  "story_title": "stan-lee-essay",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: display=block; fontsize=extralarge; fonttype=bold; text=Stan Lee Essay]\n\nThis is a defense of writer/editor Stan Lee and his place in history, <i>especially</i> during the Silver and Bronze Ages of Marvel Comics.\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[SPAN: fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=First Heading (FIXME)]\n\n\nAs an observation only, it's clear that the list of regular pencillers available to Marvel seem to consist of Jack Kirby, Steve Ditko, Don Heck, Dick Ayers and Larry Lieber (who only does back-up stories), with an occasional assist or guest spot here and there (e.g. Bill Everett on <i>Daredevil</i> this month).&nbsp; As for writers, it continues to look like Stan Lee provides <i>all</i> the plots, with scripters often being needed to actually put words on the page.&nbsp; Obviously the various Marvel artists participate in the plot of the story as well when they draw out the action and guide the characters along - per the \"Marvel Method.\"\n\n&lt;begin rant&gt;\nI will go on early record here to point out that the people who criticize Stan Lee and believe that Jack Kirby and Steve Ditko deserve equal share in the establishment of the early Marvel success are misguided.&nbsp; I <i>do</i> indeed believe that Kirby and Ditko played a <i>huge</i> role in the creation of the mainstay Marvel characters - Kirby in particular with his outstanding story-telling techniques and interesting ideas - and that Stan Lee <i>absolutely</i> could not have done it without them.&nbsp; (I state that to be <i>very</i> clear here.)\n\nBut only one guy was overseeing everything, working with <i>all</i> the artists and keeping watch over the <i>entire</i> operation, and that guy was Stan Lee.&nbsp; Do I believe he took credit for more than he was probably deserving of?&nbsp; Yes, I do, particularly with the notion of \"co-creators\" - I think he should have verbally acknowledged that he was <b>co</b>-creator and not <b>sole</b> creator.&nbsp; I think he struggled to understand that in the end.&nbsp; But time and again, he <b>co</b>-created characters with <i>this</i> artist and <i>that</i> artist, and the characters still got created without any one artist able to reasonably claim (retroactively) all, or most, of the credit.\n\nBut there is no <i>question</i> that the Marvel Universe would not exist were it not for him.&nbsp; No matter who was co-creator, Stan Lee was always involved somehow.&nbsp; He was the one driving force behind it all, and anyone who suggests otherwise just hasn't read these old books, I suspect.&nbsp; At the <i>moment</i> (early in 1964), the number of artists is rather minimal, but it won't be long before he's juggling stories with Gene Colan, John Romita, and others that follow even them - go listen to what <i>they</i> have to say (along with Don Heck) - the whole problem revolves about Kirby and Ditko, with not many others having much to say.\n\nStan was the main writer, period, and to suggest that he would never have been able to do it with different artists seems short-sighted.&nbsp; <i>Yes</i>, they were exceptionally talented, but you don't have to look beyond what Stan did with the <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> (after Ditko left) to see that the quality didn't drop just because he had to start working with a <i>different</i> talented artist (John Romita).&nbsp; The book's popularity continued to soar despite the loss of one of its co-creators.\n\nRoy Thomas has gone on record as well as saying that Stan operated in such a way as to keep the artists working so they could get paid (implying that it freed him up also so that he could then turn to work with the next artist and make sure everything kept moving forward - all the while knowing that his typewriter awaited him once he had the pages in hand).&nbsp; And Flo Steinburg also remembers the plotting sessions that Stan and Jack would have in the office, so they <i>clearly</i> worked together.\n\nIt irritates me to see Stan so denigrated in the various writings and articles that have been put forth since his death.&nbsp; Many of them seem to paint Stan as some kind of monster, which seems preposterous - those characters didn't create themselves, and those stories didn't write themselves either!\n&lt;/end rant&gt;"
}